1
00:00:00,100 --> 00:00:01,975
Three hundred million volts.

2
00:00:01,975 --> 00:00:04,921
Thirty thousand amps. Your wall socket gives

3
00:00:04,921 --> 00:00:07,532
you about one hundred and twenty volts.

4
00:00:07,532 --> 00:00:09,340
You just got the whole sky.

5
00:00:09,890 --> 00:00:12,221
The air around the bolt heats to around

6
00:00:12,221 --> 00:00:14,253
fifty thousand degrees Fahrenheit.

7
00:00:14,253 --> 00:00:16,584
That's about five times hotter than the

8
00:00:16,584 --> 00:00:17,720
surface of the Sun.

9
00:00:18,270 --> 00:00:21,113
And yet, about nine out of ten people struck

10
00:00:21,113 --> 00:00:22,470
by lightning survive.

11
00:00:23,020 --> 00:00:25,094
That sounds like the good news.

12
00:00:25,094 --> 00:00:27,769
It isn't, really. Because what lightning

13
00:00:27,769 --> 00:00:30,646
does to the survivors is the strangest part

14
00:00:30,646 --> 00:00:31,984
of this whole story.

15
00:00:31,984 --> 00:00:34,860
Let's go back thirty minutes, to the moment

16
00:00:34,860 --> 00:00:36,800
you made a terrible decision.

17
00:00:37,350 --> 00:00:38,903
It's a summer afternoon.

18
00:00:38,903 --> 00:00:40,455
You're on a golf course.

19
00:00:40,455 --> 00:00:43,301
There's a dark cloud way off on the horizon.

20
00:00:43,301 --> 00:00:45,630
It isn't even raining where you are.

21
00:00:46,180 --> 00:00:48,641
You hear a low rumble of thunder.

22
00:00:48,641 --> 00:00:50,952
You think, eh, it's miles away.

23
00:00:50,952 --> 00:00:52,220
Three more holes.

24
00:00:52,770 --> 00:00:55,450
Here's the problem. Lightning often strikes

25
00:00:55,450 --> 00:00:57,942
more than three miles from the centre of

26
00:00:57,942 --> 00:00:59,812
a storm, far outside the rain.

27
00:00:59,812 --> 00:01:02,429
So-called bolts from the blue can land ten

28
00:01:02,429 --> 00:01:03,800
to fifteen miles away.

29
00:01:04,350 --> 00:01:07,126
The National Weather Service puts it bluntly.

30
00:01:07,126 --> 00:01:09,532
If you can hear thunder, even a distant

31
00:01:09,532 --> 00:01:11,630
rumble, you are already in danger.

32
00:01:12,180 --> 00:01:15,013
And standing in an open field, as the tallest

33
00:01:15,013 --> 00:01:17,909
thing around, holding a long stick, is exactly

34
00:01:17,909 --> 00:01:20,050
how you make yourself interesting.

35
00:01:20,600 --> 00:01:23,064
There are five main ways lightning gets

36
00:01:23,064 --> 00:01:24,391
you. A direct strike.

37
00:01:24,391 --> 00:01:27,170
A side flash, where it hits a tree and jumps

38
00:01:27,170 --> 00:01:29,318
to the person sheltering under it.

39
00:01:29,318 --> 00:01:32,035
Standing under a tree is the second leading

40
00:01:32,035 --> 00:01:33,930
cause of lightning casualties.

41
00:01:34,480 --> 00:01:36,815
Ground current, where the energy spreads

42
00:01:36,815 --> 00:01:38,158
out through the ground.

43
00:01:38,158 --> 00:01:40,551
That one causes the most lightning deaths

44
00:01:40,551 --> 00:01:43,178
and injuries, because it covers a much bigger

45
00:01:43,178 --> 00:01:43,470
area.

46
00:01:44,020 --> 00:01:46,311
Conduction, through wires and pipes.

47
00:01:46,311 --> 00:01:48,985
That's how people get hurt indoors, in the

48
00:01:48,985 --> 00:01:50,831
shower, or on a corded phone.

49
00:01:50,831 --> 00:01:53,568
And streamers, little sparks of electricity

50
00:01:53,568 --> 00:01:56,050
rising off the ground to meet the bolt.

51
00:01:56,600 --> 00:01:58,842
Today, you go for the classic.

52
00:01:58,842 --> 00:02:02,504
A direct strike. The rarest kind, but potentially

53
00:02:02,504 --> 00:02:03,550
the deadliest.

54
00:02:04,100 --> 00:02:06,923
The bolt hits you, and something surprising

55
00:02:06,923 --> 00:02:09,549
happens. A lot of the current doesn't go

56
00:02:09,549 --> 00:02:12,176
through you. It races along the outside,

57
00:02:12,176 --> 00:02:13,489
just over your skin.

58
00:02:13,489 --> 00:02:15,130
This is called flashover.

59
00:02:15,680 --> 00:02:18,254
That's one reason lightning burns are usually

60
00:02:18,254 --> 00:02:19,798
not as bad as you'd expect.

61
00:02:19,798 --> 00:02:22,201
The real damage is done by the part of the

62
00:02:22,201 --> 00:02:24,717
current that goes inside, through your heart

63
00:02:24,717 --> 00:02:26,090
and your nervous system.

64
00:02:26,640 --> 00:02:29,707
Your heart stops. Doctors call it asystole.

65
00:02:29,707 --> 00:02:32,703
The electrical rhythm that runs your heart

66
00:02:32,703 --> 00:02:33,630
just, resets.

67
00:02:34,180 --> 00:02:36,549
And the area of your brain that controls

68
00:02:36,549 --> 00:02:38,385
breathing gets knocked out too.

69
00:02:38,385 --> 00:02:39,510
You stop breathing.

70
00:02:40,060 --> 00:02:41,454
Now here's the twist.

71
00:02:41,454 --> 00:02:44,242
Your heart has its own built-in pacemaker,

72
00:02:44,242 --> 00:02:46,831
and the asystole may resolve by itself,

73
00:02:46,831 --> 00:02:49,752
fairly quickly. Your heart can start beating

74
00:02:49,752 --> 00:02:50,880
again on its own.

75
00:02:51,430 --> 00:02:53,879
But breathing often stays switched off for

76
00:02:53,879 --> 00:02:56,444
longer. So your heart is back, beating away,

77
00:02:56,444 --> 00:02:58,484
pumping blood with no oxygen in it.

78
00:02:58,484 --> 00:03:00,758
That's what can kill you, a few minutes

79
00:03:00,758 --> 00:03:03,090
after you survived the actual lightning.

80
00:03:03,640 --> 00:03:06,788
So if you ever see this happen, do something.

81
00:03:06,788 --> 00:03:09,516
Call emergency services, start CPR, use

82
00:03:09,516 --> 00:03:11,685
a defibrillator if there's one.

83
00:03:11,685 --> 00:03:14,763
And no, the victim doesn't stay electrified.

84
00:03:14,763 --> 00:03:17,701
The human body does not store electricity.

85
00:03:17,701 --> 00:03:19,380
It's safe to touch them.

86
00:03:19,930 --> 00:03:22,640
Imagine dying because everyone was too scared

87
00:03:22,640 --> 00:03:24,988
to touch you. That's how much this myth

88
00:03:24,988 --> 00:03:25,470
matters.

89
00:03:26,020 --> 00:03:28,900
You wake up. But you can't move your legs.

90
00:03:28,900 --> 00:03:31,780
They're pale, maybe bluish, and nobody can

91
00:03:31,780 --> 00:03:33,220
find a pulse in them.

92
00:03:33,770 --> 00:03:36,751
It's called keraunoparalysis, a temporary

93
00:03:36,751 --> 00:03:38,932
paralysis caused by lightning.

94
00:03:38,932 --> 00:03:42,058
Terrifying. But it usually goes away within

95
00:03:42,058 --> 00:03:42,930
a few hours.

96
00:03:43,480 --> 00:03:44,944
Your ears are ringing.

97
00:03:44,944 --> 00:03:47,340
The blast can rupture your eardrums.

98
00:03:47,890 --> 00:03:50,794
And on your skin, there's something beautiful.

99
00:03:50,794 --> 00:03:53,382
A pattern like a fern, or the branches of

100
00:03:53,382 --> 00:03:55,591
a tree, spreading across your body.

101
00:03:55,591 --> 00:03:57,800
They're called Lichtenberg figures.

102
00:03:58,350 --> 00:04:01,064
They usually disappear within about twenty-four

103
00:04:01,064 --> 00:04:03,373
hours. Lightning gives you a tattoo, and

104
00:04:03,373 --> 00:04:04,470
then takes it back.

105
00:04:05,020 --> 00:04:07,400
So you're alive. You can walk.

106
00:04:07,400 --> 00:04:08,908
The burns are mild.

107
00:04:08,908 --> 00:04:12,241
The doctors run their scans, and the scans

108
00:04:12,241 --> 00:04:14,621
come back normal. You go home.

109
00:04:14,621 --> 00:04:15,970
Lucky you. Right?

110
00:04:16,520 --> 00:04:18,679
Here's what most people get wrong.

111
00:04:18,679 --> 00:04:21,537
Lightning is mainly an injury to your nervous

112
00:04:21,537 --> 00:04:23,760
system. Your brain and your nerves.

113
00:04:24,310 --> 00:04:25,980
You get headaches. Nausea.

114
00:04:25,980 --> 00:04:28,677
You struggle to learn new information, and

115
00:04:28,677 --> 00:04:30,411
to pull up old information.

116
00:04:30,411 --> 00:04:33,108
You can't remember names, or what you were

117
00:04:33,108 --> 00:04:34,970
supposed to be doing at work.

118
00:04:35,520 --> 00:04:37,389
Your sleep goes strange too.

119
00:04:37,389 --> 00:04:40,193
Sometimes you sleep way too much at first,

120
00:04:40,193 --> 00:04:43,130
and later only two or three hours at a time.

121
00:04:43,680 --> 00:04:44,987
And the worst part?

122
00:04:44,987 --> 00:04:47,531
The x-rays and MRIs come back normal.

123
00:04:47,531 --> 00:04:50,282
Like a concussion, the problem is in how

124
00:04:50,282 --> 00:04:53,032
your brain works, not in how it looks on

125
00:04:53,032 --> 00:04:53,720
a picture.

126
00:04:54,270 --> 00:04:56,741
Your friends see the same person on the

127
00:04:56,741 --> 00:04:59,338
outside. They don't understand why you're

128
00:04:59,338 --> 00:05:01,176
different. Some stop calling.

129
00:05:01,176 --> 00:05:04,090
Some survivors pull away out of embarrassment.

130
00:05:04,640 --> 00:05:07,065
Of the people who survive a lightning strike,

131
00:05:07,065 --> 00:05:09,221
about three in four have health problems

132
00:05:09,221 --> 00:05:09,760
that last.

133
00:05:10,310 --> 00:05:12,250
And some injuries show up late.

134
00:05:12,250 --> 00:05:14,816
Cataracts, a clouding of the lens in your

135
00:05:14,816 --> 00:05:17,382
eye, can appear more than a year after an

136
00:05:17,382 --> 00:05:19,260
otherwise uneventful recovery.

137
00:05:19,810 --> 00:05:23,220
Now, if you're thinking, once in a lifetime,

138
00:05:23,220 --> 00:05:26,010
fine. Let me introduce Roy Sullivan.

139
00:05:26,560 --> 00:05:29,153
Roy was a park ranger at Shenandoah National

140
00:05:29,153 --> 00:05:31,747
Park in Virginia. Between nineteen forty-two

141
00:05:31,747 --> 00:05:34,163
and nineteen seventy-seven, he was struck

142
00:05:34,163 --> 00:05:35,637
by lightning seven times.

143
00:05:35,637 --> 00:05:37,994
Guinness World Records recognises him as

144
00:05:37,994 --> 00:05:40,470
the person struck the most recorded times.

145
00:05:41,020 --> 00:05:43,743
Strike one, nineteen forty-two, at a new

146
00:05:43,743 --> 00:05:46,466
fire lookout tower that had no lightning

147
00:05:46,466 --> 00:05:49,598
rod. It burned off his eyebrows and eyelashes,

148
00:05:49,598 --> 00:05:51,300
and set his hair on fire.

149
00:05:51,850 --> 00:05:54,890
Later strikes hit him in his own front yard,

150
00:05:54,890 --> 00:05:56,824
and inside a ranger station.

151
00:05:56,824 --> 00:05:58,689
His hair caught fire again.

152
00:05:58,689 --> 00:05:59,380
And again.

153
00:05:59,930 --> 00:06:02,677
By the end, he believed storm clouds were

154
00:06:02,677 --> 00:06:05,491
following him. Once, he saw a cloud, tried

155
00:06:05,491 --> 00:06:08,373
to run away from it, and got struck anyway.

156
00:06:08,373 --> 00:06:11,187
He started carrying a can of water around,

157
00:06:11,187 --> 00:06:13,130
in case his hair caught fire.

158
00:06:13,680 --> 00:06:16,337
People started avoiding him, scared they'd

159
00:06:16,337 --> 00:06:18,362
get hit just by standing nearby.

160
00:06:18,362 --> 00:06:21,272
Which, if you think about it, is the loneliest

161
00:06:21,272 --> 00:06:23,550
possible side effect of being lucky.

162
00:06:24,100 --> 00:06:26,548
So here's how to keep your vitals boring.

163
00:06:26,548 --> 00:06:29,475
There's no safe place outdoors in a thunderstorm.

164
00:06:29,475 --> 00:06:32,281
The only properly safe places are a substantial

165
00:06:32,281 --> 00:06:34,670
building or a hard topped metal vehicle.

166
00:06:35,220 --> 00:06:37,874
Crouching down doesn't make you safe.

167
00:06:37,874 --> 00:06:40,599
Lying flat makes ground current worse.

168
00:06:40,599 --> 00:06:42,822
And don't shelter under a tree.

169
00:06:42,822 --> 00:06:43,970
Just get inside.

170
00:06:44,520 --> 00:06:46,702
And lightning absolutely does strike the

171
00:06:46,702 --> 00:06:49,048
same place twice. The Empire State Building

172
00:06:49,048 --> 00:06:51,340
gets hit around twenty-three times a year.

173
00:06:51,890 --> 00:06:54,659
The good news. In the United States, lightning

174
00:06:54,659 --> 00:06:57,608
deaths have dropped a lot, from about forty-three

175
00:06:57,608 --> 00:07:00,016
a year on average over three decades, to

176
00:07:00,016 --> 00:07:02,363
about twenty-seven a year in the decade

177
00:07:02,363 --> 00:07:05,072
to twenty eighteen. Your odds of being struck

178
00:07:05,072 --> 00:07:07,720
in a given year are around one in a million.

179
00:07:08,270 --> 00:07:11,068
But over a whole lifetime, it's about one

180
00:07:11,068 --> 00:07:13,865
in fifteen thousand. And for every person

181
00:07:13,865 --> 00:07:16,663
struck, around ten people around them are

182
00:07:16,663 --> 00:07:17,550
affected too.

183
00:07:18,100 --> 00:07:20,781
So, lightning doesn't usually kill you.

184
00:07:20,781 --> 00:07:24,149
It stops your heart, switches off your breathing,

185
00:07:24,149 --> 00:07:27,312
paints you like a fern, scrambles your memory,

186
00:07:27,312 --> 00:07:30,199
and then sends you home with a clean scan.

187
00:07:30,199 --> 00:07:32,880
Survival, it turns out, is complicated.

188
00:07:33,430 --> 00:07:36,099
When thunder roars, go indoors.

189
00:07:36,099 --> 00:07:38,166
Not after the next hole.

190
00:07:38,166 --> 00:07:38,510
Now.

191
00:07:39,060 --> 00:07:40,971
Next week, you eat nothing but fast food

192
00:07:40,971 --> 00:07:42,835
for thirty days. We'll check your liver

193
00:07:42,835 --> 00:07:44,794
every week, and the scariest result shows

194
00:07:44,794 --> 00:07:45,989
up a year after you stop.

195
00:07:45,989 --> 00:07:46,610
See you then.
